<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01974</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fc696584-2dd3-4c51-b69d-0c1b355a48e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00001">For 1 - 4, use the following information:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00002"><quote id="quote-00001">A parabola crosses the \(x\)-axis at (-1,0) and (2,0). We want to write a quadratic equation with those \(x\)-intercepts. We can do this without graphing.</quote></para><para id="para-00003">When \(y=0\), what must \(x\) be?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00005">We know that (-1,0) and (2,0) are points on the graph when \(y=0\). So there are two possibilities for \(x\): \(x=-1\) and \(x=2\).</para>
    
    <para id="para-00006">\(x=-1\) and \(x=2\) are called the real solutions of the quadratic because they are the solutions of the equation when we set \(y=0\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00007">Rewrite the equations from number 1 so they both equal zero.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00009">\(x+1=0\) and \(x-2=0\).</para>
    
    <para id="para-00010">Because each of the equations equals zero, multiplying them together will also equal zero.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00011">What equation do you get when you multiply the previous equations together?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00013">\((x+1)(x-2)=0 \)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00014">Multiply the binomials.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00016">\(x^2-x-2=0\)</para>
    
    <para id="para-00017">This answer can also give us the more general quadratic with those \(x\)-intercepts. We replace 0 with \(y\) to show that \(y\) varies and isn’t always 0. So, \(y=x^2-x-2\) is a parabola that crosses the \(x\)-axis at -1 and 2.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
</section>

<note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
  <para id="para-00018">The solution to a quadratic equation is \(x = 4\) and \(x = - 8\). What is the equation?</para>
</note>

<section id="sect-00002"><para id="para-00019">When you write a quadratic in factored form, that helps you find the \(x\)-intercepts. 
  \(y=x^2+2x-3\)
  \(y=(x+3)(x-1)\)
</para>
  <para id="para-00020">When we set \(y=0\), we find the places where the parabola crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00021">\(0=(x+3)(x-1)\)</para>
  
  <para id="para-00022">So, \(0=x+3\) and \(0=x-1\)
  \(x=-3\) and 1.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00023">-3 and 1 are called the real solutions to the quadratic equation.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00024">We can also start with the real solutions and use them to write quadratic equations.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00025">When we know that \(x=-3\) and 1, then that means \(0=x+3\) and \(0=x-1\).</para>
  
  <para id="para-00026">So, \(0=(x+3)(x-1)\).</para>
  
  <para id="para-00027">And, using the FOIL method to multiply this out, we come to 
  \(0=x^2+2x-3.\)</para>
  
  <para id="para-00028">When \(y\) is not equal to zero, we have the following quadratic equation</para>
  
  <para id="para-00029">\(y=x^2+2x-3\), which is exactly where we started!</para>

  
  <!-- BEGIN TRY IT-->

  <note class="try" id="note-00002">
<title>Try It: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
<!--Q#1-->
<exercise id="exer-00005">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
  
    <para id="para-00030">Write a quadratic from the real solutions \(x=1\) and \(x=7\) without graphing.</para>
  <para id="para-00031">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
<para id="para-00032">Compare your answer: 
\(x^2-8x+7\)</para>

<para id="para-00033">\(x=1\) and \(x=7\) become \(x-1=0\) and \(x-7=0\). \(y=(x-1)(x-7)= x^2-x-7x+7= x^2-8x+7\).</para>

</solution></exercise></note>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TRY IT --></section>
